    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “Puncture wounds with potential crush-laceration injury”. A dog's canine tooth is pointed and is driven by the crushing force of the jaw, so it punctures deeply and then tears the tissue as the animal shakes or withdraws. That produces a small surface wound over a deep tract, and the tract carries oral flora such as Pasteurella multocida, anaerobes and, if the animal is rabid, rabies virus down into poorly vascularised tissue where infection readily establishes. Bites are therefore classified as puncture wounds with potential crush-laceration. Why the other options fail: clean incisional wounds are cut by a sharp instrument under controlled conditions and have neat, apposed edges (A); abrasions are superficial friction injuries that graze the surface without penetrating (B); and a closed contusion is blunt trauma with the skin left intact, whereas a bite breaches it (D).",
