    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Eliminate juices and snacks in the hours immediately preceding scheduled mealtimes”. Toddlers grow far more slowly than infants, so appetite falls in what is called the physiologic anorexia of toddlerhood; it is normal, not a failure. Sweet drinks and snacks are energy-dense and fill a small stomach, so withdrawing them for an hour or two before a meal means the child arrives hungry and eats the balanced food offered, in a portion small enough to finish. Why the other options fail: doubling whole milk adds calories, displaces iron-containing solids and can cause iron-deficiency anaemia (A); forcing a child to sit until the plate is empty turns food into a power struggle and breeds aversion (B); and multivitamin syrups do not create appetite, and some are toxic in overdose (C).",
