    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Playing regularly in unpaved roadside soil alongside heavy vehicular exhaust traffic”. Tetraethyl lead from petrol is deposited in topsoil along busy roads and persists there for years, and unpaved ground stays as dust that is easily disturbed. Toddlers put hands and objects in their mouths, so they swallow the contaminated soil and dust directly; their developing nervous systems and higher absorption per kilogram make lead especially harmful to them. Why the other options fail: plastic balls are not a recognised lead source and carry no exhaust residue (A); clean indoor grass in an athletic facility has no contaminated soil or traffic dust (C); and clean cotton stuffed toys are neither soiled nor finished with lead-containing paint (D).",
    strategy: "Environmental health link: road dust and heavy traffic soil contain high lead concentrations, easily ingested by toddlers with hand-to-mouth habits.",
