    categorySlug: "med-surg",
    stem: "A 10-year-old boy in Ghana develops jaundice, dark urine, and acute pallor after receiving an antimalarial drug. Testing reveals an inborn error of metabolism causing premature red blood cell destruction. Which disorder is most likely?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Coeliac disease, an autoimmune reaction to dietary gluten"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Phenylketonuria (PKU), an inborn error of amino-acid metabolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Homocystinuria, an inborn error of methionine metabolism"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency”. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is the first and rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway, and it regenerates the NADPH that keeps glutathione reduced and neutralises oxidative stress inside the red cell. When the enzyme is deficient the cell cannot defend itself against an oxidant drug such as primaquine or a sulfonamide, fava beans, or severe infection; haemoglobin denatures and precipitates as Heinz bodies, and the cells are destroyed intravascularly. The result is the abrupt jaundice, dark urine from haemoglobinuria and sudden pallor seen here, in a boy, because the gene is X-linked. Why the other options fail: coeliac disease is a gluten-sensitive enteropathy causing diarrhoea and malabsorption, not haemolysis (A); phenylketonuria is a defect of phenylalanine hydroxylase that causes intellectual disability if untreated, not anaemia (B); and homocystinuria produces lens dislocation, thrombosis and a marfanoid habitus (D).",
    strategy: "High-yield West African clinical link: G6PD deficiency + oxidative medication (or fava beans) = acute hemolytic anemia with dark urine and jaundice.",
