    categorySlug: "med-surg",
    stem: "A patient recovering from subtotal thyroidectomy develops tingling in the fingers and a positive Chvostek's sign due to accidental parathyroid injury. Which laboratory profile is expected in hypoparathyroidism?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Increased serum calcium with decreased serum phosphate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Decreased serum calcium with increased serum phosphate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Decreased serum calcium with decreased serum phosphate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal serum calcium with elevated alkaline phosphatase only"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Decreased serum calcium with increased serum phosphate”. Parathyroid hormone raises serum calcium by mobilising it from bone, increasing renal tubular reabsorption and activating vitamin D, and at the same time it promotes phosphate excretion by the kidney. When the parathyroids are damaged or removed at thyroidectomy both actions are lost: calcium falls, producing perioral tingling, carpopedal spasm and a positive Chvostek's or Trousseau's sign, while phosphate rises because the kidney can no longer excrete it. Why the other options fail: high calcium with low phosphate is the pattern of hyperparathyroidism, the opposite of this injury (A); low calcium with low phosphate points to vitamin D deficiency or malabsorption rather than loss of PTH (C); and normal calcium with an isolated raised alkaline phosphatase is the pattern of Paget's disease or bone healing (D).",
    strategy: "Remember the seesaw rule: Calcium and Phosphate have an inverse relationship in parathyroid disease. Hypoparathyroidism = Low Calcium, High Phosphate.",
