    stem: "A patient is evaluated for primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison's disease). Which clinical sign is NOT expected in this condition?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Hyperpigmentation of palmar creases and scars"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Postural hypotension and dizziness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hirsutism and central moon face"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypoglycemia and chronic fatigue"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “Hirsutism and central moon face”. Addison's disease is destruction of the adrenal cortex, so cortisol, aldosterone and adrenal androgens all fall together. Without aldosterone the kidney loses sodium and retains potassium, giving postural hypotension and dizziness; without cortisol there is hypoglycaemia, weight loss and chronic fatigue; and without androgen feedback the pituitary drives ACTH and, with it, melanocyte-stimulating hormone up, which produces the bronze hyperpigmentation of palmar creases, scars and buccal mucosa. Hirsutism and a central moon face are the opposite picture. Why the other options fail: hyperpigmentation of creases and scars (A), postural hypotension with dizziness (B) and hypoglycaemia with chronic fatigue (D) are all expected in primary adrenal insufficiency, so none of them is the answer to a question asking what is not.",
    strategy: "Contrast Addison's and Cushing's: Addison's has low steroids (thin, low BP, bronze skin, hair loss); Cushing's has high steroids (moon face, hirsutism, high BP).",
    difficulty: "easy",
    tags: ["endocrine", "addisons-disease", "assessment"],
    isFree: true,
