    "correctChoiceId": "d",
    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Intrahepatic cholestasis causing impaired biliary excretion into the duodenum”. In acute hepatitis the hepatocytes are swollen and the small intrahepatic bile canaliculi are narrowed by inflammation, so bile cannot drain into the duodenum — intrahepatic cholestasis. Bilirubin never reaches the gut to be converted to stercobilin, which is what gives stool its normal brown colour, so the stool becomes pale and clay-coloured; and because bile salts are also missing from the lumen, dietary fat is not emulsified and is passed in bulky, greasy, offensive stools. Why the other options fail: intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells and has nothing to do with stool colour (A); increased colonic water reabsorption would make stool harder and drier, not pale and bulky (B); and a change in gut bacteria does not remove the bile pigment that gives stool its colour (C).",
    strategy: "Hepatitis presentation: liver canaliculi swell -> bile cannot enter bowel -> stool turns pale and fatty (bulky).",
