    categorySlug: "safety-infection-control",
    stem: "A nurse examines a school-age child presenting with intense nocturnal pruritus. Inspection of the finger webs and wrists reveals fine, thread-like wavy ridges beneath the epidermis. Which diagnosis does the nurse suspect?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Tinea versicolor"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scabies infestation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Impetigo contagiosa"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atopic eczema"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Scabies infestation”. The female Sarcoptes scabiei mite burrows into the stratum corneum and tunnels forward, laying eggs and faeces along her track, and that tunnel shows through the skin as a fine, wavy, thread-like line. The sites are the warm flexures — finger webs, wrists, axillae, belt line, genitalia — and the itching is an allergic response to the mite and its products, so it is most intense at night. Why the other options fail: tinea versicolor is a Malassezia infection producing scaly, faintly discoloured macules on the trunk and shoulders, with no burrows (A); impetigo is a superficial infection with honey-coloured crusts (C); and atopic eczema gives ill-defined dry, inflamed, itchy patches in the flexures of an atopic child, without thread-like tracks (D).",
