    categorySlug: "med-surg",
    stem: "A client develops a localized, red, itchy patch of mild contact dermatitis on the forearm after using a new cosmetic cream. What initial topical pharmacotherapy is recommended for symptomatic relief?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Apply an aggressive keratolytic salicylic acid ointment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scrub the area vigorously with undiluted antiseptic soap"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a mild, low-potency topical corticosteroid cream (such as 1% hydrocortisone)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply topical antiviral medication three times daily"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “Apply a mild, low-potency topical corticosteroid cream (such as 1% hydrocortisone)”. Mild contact dermatitis is a localised delayed hypersensitivity reaction, and a low-potency corticosteroid applied thinly for a short course suppresses the cutaneous inflammation and relieves the itch once the offending cream is stopped. The lowest potencies are used because they are effective on thin skin and carry little risk of atrophy. Why the other options fail: salicylic acid is a keratolytic for thick scaly lesions such as warts and calluses and would irritate inflamed skin (A); scrubbing with undiluted antiseptic soap damages the epidermal barrier and worsens the dermatitis (B); and topical antivirals treat herpes simplex or zoster and have no role in an eczematous reaction (D).",
