    categorySlug: "maternal-newborn",
    stem: "A 26-year-old woman is evaluated for polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS). Which clinical feature is NOT typically associated with this syndrome?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Insulin resistance and weight gain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chronic anovulation and infertility"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hirsutism, acne, and oily skin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Significant involuntary weight loss"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "d",
    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Significant involuntary weight loss”. Polycystic ovarian syndrome rests on three features: excess androgen, chronic anovulation and metabolic insulin resistance. The excess androgen produces hirsutism, acne and oily skin; the anovulation produces irregular or absent periods and infertility; and the insulin resistance drives weight gain and central obesity, which in turn worsen the hyperandrogenism. Significant involuntary weight loss is not part of the syndrome at all. Why the other options fail: insulin resistance with weight gain (A), chronic anovulation with infertility (B) and hirsutism with acne and oily skin (C) are all typical of polycystic ovarian syndrome, so none of them is the answer to a question asking what is not.",
    strategy: "Notice the exception: PCOS is strongly linked to weight gain and insulin resistance, not weight loss.",
    difficulty: "easy",
    tags: ["gynaecology", "pcos", "endocrine", "reproductive-health"],
    isFree: true,
