    stem: "A 42-year-old woman presents to the clinic with persistent cold intolerance, coarse dry skin, generalized fatigue, and a 5-kg weight gain despite poor appetite. Which endocrine disorder is most consistent with these findings?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Thyrotoxicosis (hyperthyroidism)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Primary hypothyroidism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Type 1 diabetes mellitus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pheochromocytoma"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Primary hypothyroidism”. In primary hypothyroidism the thyroid gland itself fails, so thyroxine and tri-iodothyronine fall and the pituitary drives TSH up. Every tissue slows: the basal metabolic rate drops, producing cold intolerance, fatigue, bradycardia and constipation, while coarse dry skin and weight gain occur even though intake is poor, because metabolism is depressed rather than appetite raised. Why the other options fail: thyrotoxicosis causes the opposite picture of heat intolerance and weight loss with a preserved or increased appetite (A); type 1 diabetes presents with polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss from insulin deficiency, not weight gain (C); and pheochromocytoma gives episodic hypertension, palpitations and sweating from catecholamine surges, without this diffuse slowing (D).",
