    "correctChoiceId": "d",
    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Applying ice packs and performing vigorous deep tissue massage to the lower extremities”. Paget's disease is chaotic osteoclastic resorption followed by disorganised bone formation, so the bone is expanded but mechanically weak and prone to pathologic fracture. Cold stiffens already painful, spastic muscle, and vigorous deep massage over that fragile bone can break it, so both parts of this action are unsafe and must be stopped at once. Why the other options fail: gentle range-of-motion work with physiotherapy preserves mobility without loading the bone abnormally (A); bisphosphonates are the treatment of choice because they suppress osteoclastic activity and reduce pain (B); and mild superficial warmth is the correct comfort measure, because heat relaxes the muscle spasm that accompanies Pagetic bone pain (C).",
    strategy: "Pain and safety in Paget's disease: Use superficial heat, not ice, for comfort; avoid deep, aggressive massage over fragile deformed bones.",
