    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “A private isolation room close to the nursing station under droplet precautions”. Neisseria meningitidis is spread in large respiratory droplets, so the patient needs a single room with droplet precautions — a surgical mask within three feet, and a mask on the patient for transport — until at least 24 hours of effective intravenous antibiotic have been given. A room close to the nursing station is chosen as well, because this patient can deteriorate abruptly, with seizures, falling consciousness or rising intracranial pressure, and needs to be seen quickly. Why the other options fail: sharing with a patient being treated for viral meningitis exposes that patient to meningococcal droplets and gives no protection to either (A); an open four-bed ward beside postoperative patients exposes everyone in it (B); and a room at the far end of the corridor is unsafe for a patient who may deteriorate suddenly and seizure (D).",
    strategy: "Isolation + Prioritisation: Bacterial meningitis requires Droplet Precautions in a private room AND close proximity to the nurses' station for frequent neurological checks.",
