    categorySlug: "gerontology-community",
    stem: "An 74-year-old woman attends the community audiology clinic. The nurse suspects presbycusis based on which presenting complaint?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Ability to hear whispers but inability to hear low bass notes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sudden unilateral total hearing loss following waking"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Continuous fluid discharge and severe pain in the left ear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Progressive difficulty hearing high-pitched voices and telephone chimes"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "d",
    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Progressive difficulty hearing high-pitched voices and telephone chimes”. Presbycusis is the bilateral, slowly progressive sensorineural hearing loss of ageing. Hair cells in the basal turn of the cochlea, the part that encodes high frequencies, are lost first, so high-pitched sounds go earliest: women's and children's voices, the consonants s, f, th and k, telephone rings and chimes. Speech is heard but not understood, especially against background noise. Why the other options fail: hearing whispers but not low bass notes is the reverse pattern and does not fit basal hair cell loss (A); sudden unilateral total loss is idiopathic sudden sensorineural hearing loss, an emergency unrelated to ageing (B); and discharge with severe pain indicates infection or perforation (C).",
    strategy: "Presbycusis key sign: Age-related loss of high-pitched sounds first. Low-pitched sounds remain preserved until later stages.",
