    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "At a noisy religious gathering with loud musical accompaniment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "In a crowded market or busy shopping mall"}, {"id": "c", "text": "In a quiet, controlled home environment while conversing with one person"}, {"id": "d", "text": "While walking next to heavy roadway traffic"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “In a quiet, controlled home environment while conversing with one person”. A new hearing aid floods an ear that has been deprived of sound, and the brain needs graded exposure to relearn what the amplified signal means. Starting in a quiet room, with one familiar speaker and short sessions, lets the patient learn to set the volume and separate speech from background noise before facing harder listening conditions. Why the other options fail: loud music at a religious gathering mixes amplification with intense competing sound and invites feedback and fatigue (A); a crowded market delivers many overlapping voices that a new user cannot yet sort out (B); and practising beside heavy traffic adds constant noise plus a safety hazard while walking (D).",
    strategy: "Sensory adaptation principle: Start in quiet, controlled environments first; build up tolerance before going into noisy crowds.",
