    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Presence of needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals”. Acute gout is confirmed by arthrocentesis showing needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals that are negatively birefringent under polarised light. The urate crystals are what define the disease, and the fluid is inflammatory but sterile. Why the other options fail: rhomboid, positively birefringent calcium pyrophosphate crystals indicate pseudogout (B); Gram-negative intracellular diplococci indicate gonococcal septic arthritis (C); and a frankly bloody aspirate with lipid droplets suggests trauma or fat, not gout (D).",
    strategy: "Diagnostic hallmark: Gout = Needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals (negative birefringence). Pseudogout = Rhomboid calcium pyrophosphate.",
