    categorySlug: "gerontology-community",
    stem: "A community health nurse assesses pain in an 84-year-old client with osteoarthritis. What key principle should guide the nurse's evaluation of pain in older adult patients?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Older adults often underreport pain from fear of addiction or of losing independence"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Older people gradually lose their pain receptors, so they feel pain much less intensely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Severe persistent pain is a normal and expected consequence of growing older"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Older adults cannot use a numeric rating scale reliably, so their pain must be guessed"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Older adults frequently underreport pain due to fear of addiction, loss of independence, or the misconception that pain is inevitable”. Pain is never a normal part of growing older, and the older adult is at particular risk of having it undertreated. Many underreport pain because they fear addiction, fear losing independence or becoming a burden, or believe that pain is simply inevitable at their age; some also understate it or attribute it to ageing itself. The nurse must therefore screen actively, believe the report and advocate for analgesia rather than wait to be told. Why the other options fail: pain receptors are not lost with age and thresholds do not simply rise (B); persistent pain is not a healthy consequence of ageing, and left untreated it reduces mobility, sleep and function (C); and most older adults can use a simple 0 to 10 scale or a faces scale quite reliably, provided sight and hearing are accommodated (D).",
