    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Elevate the legs above heart level using pillows”. In chronic venous insufficiency the valves fail and venous pressure stays high, so fluid is forced into the tissues and collects as dependent oedema. Raising the legs above the level of the right atrium lets gravity drain the veins and lymphatics back towards the heart, lowers capillary hydrostatic pressure and allows the oedema to reabsorb; it should be combined with compression and regular walking. Why the other options fail: sitting with the legs dangling leaves the feet below the heart, so blood pools and oedema worsens (B); crossing the legs compresses the popliteal vein and obstructs venous return further (C); and lying prone with the legs hyperextended neither raises them above the heart nor protects the lower back (D).",
