    stem: "A nurse is performing a complex sterile dressing change on a client's deep surgical incision using sterile normal saline. While transferring a saturated gauze pad, the nurse accidentally allows the wet gauze to touch the patient's intact skin on the upper chest. Which action should the nurse take immediately?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Pick up the gauze with sterile forceps and place it directly into the wound bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rinse the gauze thoroughly in sterile saline and proceed with wound packing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discard the contaminated gauze pad and prepare a fresh sterile gauze sponge"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Continue using the gauze since intact patient skin has already been prepped with alcohol"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "c",
    rationale: "CORRECT (C) — “Discard the contaminated gauze pad and prepare a fresh sterile gauze sponge”. Sterility is lost the instant a sterile object touches anything that is not sterile, and intact patient skin is not sterile. Moist gauze is worse still, because fluid wicks micro-organisms through the fabric by strike-through. The pad has been contaminated, cannot be made sterile again, and must be discarded; a fresh sterile sponge is taken with sterile forceps. Why the other options fail: placing the pad in the wound carries the organisms it has just picked up from the skin straight into the wound (A); rinsing does not sterilise a contaminated sponge, and neither does alcohol prepping make skin sterile (B, D).",
