    stem: "A high school athlete sustains a direct blow to the head during a match, experiencing a brief loss of consciousness lasting 30 seconds followed by transient disorientation, amnesia of the event, and headache, with a normal non-contrast head CT scan. Which head injury has the client sustained?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Cerebral laceration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Concussion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contrecoup contusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Epidural hematoma"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Concussion”. Concussion is a traumatically induced but transient disturbance of brain function: brief loss of consciousness, confusion or disorientation, and amnesia for the event, with headache and dizziness, and no structural lesion visible on imaging. The normal CT is part of the definition rather than a contradiction of it — computed tomography is done to exclude a bleed, and a normal scan in this setting supports concussion. Why the other options fail: a cerebral laceration is physical tearing of brain tissue, with focal deficit and blood on imaging (A); a contrecoup contusion is bruising of the brain on the side opposite the impact and is visible on CT (C); and an epidural haematoma produces a biconvex collection with a lucid interval and then deterioration (D).",
