    "correctChoiceId": "d",
    rationale: "CORRECT (D) — “Pushing protruding bone ends back beneath the skin and removing bone fragments”. In an open fracture the exposed bone is left exactly as it lies at the scene. Pushing the protruding end back under the skin or lifting out loose fragments drives soil and environmental organisms deep into the medullary canal, where infection is devastating, and the sharp end can lacerate the artery, vein or nerve running alongside it. The wound is covered with a sterile or clean dressing and the limb is splinted in the position found. Why the other options fail: covering the exposed bone with a clean dressing (A), splinting the limb in the position found (B) and immobilising the joints above and below the fracture (C) are all correct first-aid actions for an open fracture.",
