    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Lateral recumbent with the neck flexed and knees drawn up to the chest (fetal position)”. The lumbar puncture needle has to pass between the spinous processes into the subarachnoid space, so the gaps between them must be opened. Lying the patient on one side with the neck flexed forward and the knees drawn up to the chest — the curled fetal position — flexes the lumbar spine, separates the spinous processes and widens the interspinous distance, which both eases entry and keeps the patient still. Why the other options fail: extension of the lumbar spine closes the interspinous gaps and brings the spinous processes together, making entry harder (B); the prone position with the head turned does not flex the lumbar spine and is reserved for patients who cannot lie on their side (C); and the lithotomy position does not open the lumbar interspaces at all (D).",
    strategy: "Lumbar puncture positioning: side-lying curled 'fetal position' or seated leaning over a bedside table opens up the spinous processes.",
