    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Engulfing and digesting invading microorganisms and cellular debris through phagocytosis”. Neutrophils are the first leukocytes to reach an acute injury, arriving in numbers within six to twelve hours. They are drawn to the site by chemotaxis, marginate along the endothelium, squeeze between the endothelial cells by diapedesis, and then engulf and digest bacteria, necrotic tissue and debris by phagocytosis — that is their defining function in the acute cellular phase. Why the other options fail: antibodies are made by B lymphocytes and plasma cells, not by neutrophils (A); collagen cross-linking is the work of fibroblasts during the proliferative phase (C); and histamine is released by mast cells and causes vasodilation, not constriction (D).",
