    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Expiratory wheezing, progressive dyspnea, and persistent chest tightness/cough”. Asthma narrows the lower airways through bronchial smooth-muscle spasm, mucosal oedema and tenacious mucus plugging. Because the airways are already narrow and the lungs are compressed during expiration, the obstruction is most marked on expiration: air is trapped, flow becomes turbulent, and the patient develops expiratory wheeze with progressive dyspnoea, tachypnoea and persistent chest tightness and cough. Why the other options fail: inspiratory stridor with a barking cough and drooling points to an upper airway obstruction such as croup or epiglottitis (A); a friction rub with haemoptysis and asymmetric expansion suggests pleurisy, pulmonary embolism or pneumothorax (C); and sonorous rhonchi that clear with coughing are secretions in the large airways, not bronchospasm (D).",
