    stem: "While auscultating the anterolateral thorax of a client reporting sharp, stabbing chest pain aggravated by deep inspiration, the nurse detects a rough, grating, creaking sound heard during both inspiration and expiration that does not clear with coughing. How should the nurse identify this sound?",
    choices: [{"id": "a", "text": "Coarse crackles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pleural friction rub"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sibilant wheezes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sonorous rhonchi"}],
    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Pleural friction rub”. When the pleura is inflamed — by infection, infarction or tumour — fibrin is deposited on both surfaces and roughens them. The visceral and parietal layers then grate over one another with every breath, producing a rough, creaking, grating sound heard in both inspiration and expiration, localised over the affected area, synchronous with the sharp pleuritic pain, and unaltered by coughing. Why the other options fail: crackles are bubbling sounds produced by air passing through fluid in the small airways and alveoli, and they are mainly inspiratory (A); sibilant wheezes are high-pitched expiratory sounds from narrowed airways (C); and sonorous rhonchi are low-pitched snoring sounds from the large airways that clear with coughing (D).",
    strategy: "Auscultation keywords: 'grating/creaking sound', 'heard in both inspiration and expiration', 'unaffected by coughing' = pleural friction rub.",
    difficulty: "easy",
    tags: ["med-surg", "respiratory", "auscultation", "breath-sounds", "pleurisy"],
    isFree: true,
