    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Bronchial asthma”. Asthma is chronic airway inflammation with bronchial hyperresponsiveness, producing episodic airflow obstruction that is largely reversible — it resolves either spontaneously or after a bronchodilator. That reversibility is the distinguishing clinical feature, and it is what separates asthma from the other obstructive diseases. Why the other options fail: bronchiectasis is permanent dilatation of the bronchi with a chronic productive cough, and the damage does not reverse (B); COPD produces progressive and largely irreversible fixed obstruction (C); and cystic fibrosis is a genetic disease of viscous secretions with progressive obstruction (D).",
    strategy: "Look for the key differentiator: 'reversible bronchial obstruction' = asthma; 'fixed/irreversible' = COPD.",
