    "correctChoiceId": "b",
    rationale: "CORRECT (B) — “Systemic accumulation and dermal deposition of excess bile salts”. In cholestatic liver disease bile cannot leave the liver, so bile salts reflux into the systemic circulation and are deposited in the skin. There they act directly on cutaneous nerve endings and produce a generalised, intractable itch that is worst at night and in older patients and that resolves only when the cholestasis is relieved — which is why it is treated with bile-acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine rather than with antihistamines. Why the other options fail: high blood glucose does not irritate cutaneous nerves in this way (A); histamine from mast cells is the mechanism of urticaria and allergic itch, not of cholestatic pruritus (C); and vitamin K deficiency causes bruising and bleeding, not itching (D).",
