    "correctChoiceId": "a",
    rationale: "CORRECT (A) — “Anterograde amnesia”. Anterograde amnesia is the inability to lay down new memories after the event that caused the injury. The hippocampus is what encodes new declarative memories, and with both hippocampi ischaemic nothing new can be registered — hence the missing breakfast and the forgotten name — while memories laid down decades earlier are already stored in the cortex and survive intact. Retrograde amnesia, by contrast, is loss of memories formed before the injury, which this patient does not have. Why the other options fail: confabulation is the filling of memory gaps with invented content, not an inability to store new memories (B); spatial disorientation is loss of the sense of place, without a failure of memory encoding (C); and retrograde amnesia is loss of memories formed before the event, which is the opposite of this patient's problem (D).",
